1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,180
In the early days of World War II, the Germans Stuka and Messerschmitt ruled the skies of Europe.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:11,280
Acting as flying artillery, these warbirds sought out prey with a merciless accuracy

3
00:00:11,281 --> 00:00:14,920
and unleashed a new word on the English language, Blitzkrieg.

4
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:19,520
Conceived in secret, perfected in battle, destroyed by its creators.

5
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:22,540
The Luftwaffe, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,200
Kamerad, Kamerad, die Losung ist heil.

7
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:14,300
Rann an den Feind, rann an den Feind, kommt das Engel an.

8
00:01:15,660 --> 00:01:19,660
Kamerad, Kamerad, der Befehl ist da, wir starten.

9
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:23,980
Kamerad, Kamerad, alle Mädels müssen warten.

10
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:29,920
Die Losung ist bekannt, ran an den Feind, ran an den Feind.

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,260
That was, of course, every day in the armed forces news came this England song, you know.

12
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:45,720
And that was, of course, how they prepared the people for really to be concerned about that.

13
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:56,538
In the final months of World War II, Luftwaffe pilots fighting against great odds took to the air to stop the Allied bomber raids against the heartland of the Third

14
00:01:56,539 --> 00:01:57,239
Reich.

15
00:01:57,240 --> 00:02:01,120
We always had to take off when they came to central Germany.

16
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:04,960
And then we were let into the bomber formation from the front.

17
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:09,680
We stayed away from rear attacks because they were too costly.

18
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:15,480
We were probably 20, 30, 109s and Pocke-Wulfs spread out.

19
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:22,680
And we went in and just parts were flying all over.

20
00:02:22,681 --> 00:02:32,240
I saw parachutes, I saw planes, fighters going into the bombers, ramming, and not willfully, you know, just that happened.

21
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:40,180
It was so crowded, you could not move left, you could not move right, and in front of you was a bomber, and it was often too late to pull up.

22
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:47,040
Now, I was lucky enough to be on the outer right-hand side in this attack, and they got me this B-17.

23
00:02:47,041 --> 00:02:58,280
I saw it was burning pretty good and then I have funny feeling and looked up half to the

24
00:02:58,281 --> 00:03:02,920
north-east of me high up mustangs coming down on me

25
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:15,840
so I concentrated on the Mustangs well I had still the chance to make a left and go down and let them

26
00:03:15,841 --> 00:03:23,360
follow me and that didn't feel right so I aimed at the first one and open up is all I had

27
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:34,640
we were damn close we passed each other the mustang exploded behind me that was nothing but a fireball

28
00:03:34,641 --> 00:03:41,120
and the rest followed me and gave me hell from every side they attacked me that they chased me

29
00:03:41,121 --> 00:03:42,447
to the center of Germany.

30
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:46,320
They hit me pretty good in the undercarriage, you know, in the left wing.

31
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:50,840
But they didn't bring me down. I made a crash landing, more or less, you know.

32
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:54,660
Any crash landing, as long as you survive, is a good one.

33
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:58,380
We were feeling pretty strong, you know.

34
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:03,000
And fear is not the right word.

35
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:08,580
You fly, you do what you have to do, and if it happens, it happens.

36
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:11,780
One day your number is up. That's how we saw it.

37
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:18,160
Throughout the war, the German airmen flew with great skill and determination.

38
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:22,700
German industry had built for them some of the best flying machines in the world.

39
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:27,620
Yet Hitler's air force had ruled the continent of Europe for not much more than a year.

40
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,980
It was an air force built for a short war by short-sighted leaders.

41
00:04:32,981 --> 00:04:43,020
The victorious Allies of the First World War signed the Treaty of Versailles in June of 1919

42
00:04:43,021 --> 00:04:45,900
and called for an end to German military aviation.

43
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:49,220
Under the supervision of an Allied control commission,

44
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:54,720
Germany was obliged to surrender all of her nearly 15,000 aircraft to the Allies.

45
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:00,320
But the treaty put only minor limitations on the development of civil aviation.

46
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:04,560
German aircraft manufacturers moved their factories out of the country,

47
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:07,480
far from the nuisance of Allied commissions.

48
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:13,180
Yonkers became a large company. They built airplanes in Russia.

49
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:17,680
Hinkle established a factory in Sweden. There was a Dornier factory in Switzerland.

50
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:23,180
And they had to build a first generation of aircraft to really relearn how to build modern aircraft.

51
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:27,340
And that was done at a considerable secret expenditure of Marx.

52
00:05:27,341 --> 00:05:36,040
In 1933, Adolf Hitler, as Germany's newly elected chancellor, further encouraged the secret development of military aircraft.

53
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:41,380
And soon began to gather the men who would fly for the Reich.

54
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:51,180
When I was 14, I had the opportunity to join the flying group of the Hitler Youth.

55
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:56,180
And that consisted of weekly workshop activity.

56
00:05:56,181 --> 00:06:01,340
Each boy had to learn how to glue gliders together.

57
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:07,880
And after so many hours of workshop time, we were then entitled to it.

58
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:13,460
It didn't cost any money, but we were entitled to participate in actual flight activity.

59
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:16,500
The first flight was a solo flight.

60
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:22,480
The first flight was for a 14-year-old boy.

61
00:06:22,481 --> 00:06:25,000
It was a fantastic achievement, you know.

62
00:06:25,001 --> 00:06:27,780
To fly, to actually pee and beer.

63
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:34,000
It was like nothing in the world felt before, you know, for us youngsters.

64
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:35,760
It was just fun.

65
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:38,320
So I was gung-ho for it.

66
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:49,040
By 1935, Hitler felt secure enough with his power to publicly announce the existence of his air weapon, the Luftwaffe.

67
00:06:49,041 --> 00:06:57,580
To command his force of 1,800 aircraft and some 20,000 men, Hitler appointed a former World War I fighter ace.

68
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:05,840
Hermann Goering was a ruthless organizer, and he had the capacity to force people to take brave decisions and take responsibility for them.

69
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:12,060
So he was able to induce manufacturers to spend a lot more money than they had any idea that they'd ever be able to spend.

70
00:07:12,061 --> 00:07:13,620
And money was no object.

71
00:07:13,621 --> 00:07:23,651
So by designing their airplanes beginning in 1934, testing them, introducing them into production in 1937, by the time the war came, Hitler had the most modern air

72
00:07:23,652 --> 00:07:24,820
force in the world.

73
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:36,031
With the power of their war machine growing, Hermann Göring and Adolf Hitler managed to intimidate European leaders and won their territorial claims to Austria and

74
00:07:36,032 --> 00:07:37,920
Czechoslovakia without a fight.

75
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:45,640
By the summer of 1939, Luftwaffe's strength stood at 3,700 aircraft and a million and a half men.

76
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:51,240
With nothing to stand in their way, Hitler and Goring turned to more violent methods.

77
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:56,560
On the morning of September 1st, under cover of heavy fog,

78
00:07:57,140 --> 00:08:01,760
soldiers of the German army moved east and rushed across the frontier border of Poland.

79
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:06,840
Armored forces stormed through the defenses as the Luftwaffe hammered all resistance.

80
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:15,920
The blitzkrieg had begun.

81
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:26,020
The rapid German advance had left behind many pockets of resistance in rear areas.

82
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:31,120
The Luftwaffe offered direct support to the army by bombing these Polish strongpoints.

83
00:08:31,121 --> 00:08:38,220
it was here that the legend of the stuka dive bomber was born

84
00:08:38,221 --> 00:08:46,700
the junkers ju-87 was a large gull-winged airplane with fixed gear and able to dive

85
00:08:46,701 --> 00:08:52,700
very close to the ground and consequently were very accurate at that time there were no level

86
00:08:52,701 --> 00:08:57,740
bombers in the world that were really effective level bombers didn't hit their targets level

87
00:08:57,741 --> 00:09:00,089
bombers often didn't find their targets.

88
00:09:00,090 --> 00:09:03,260
Whereas the Stukas were like a well-keyed-in artillery

89
00:09:03,261 --> 00:09:06,781
piece. They blasted in and would hit exactly.

90
00:09:06,782 --> 00:09:10,380
The Stukas would appear over a point precisely

91
00:09:10,381 --> 00:09:15,140
when the tanks wanted them to be, would attack, and while the enemy was still stunned from the

92
00:09:15,141 --> 00:09:18,680
concussive effect of the bombs, the armed forces would be there to exploit the opening.

93
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:26,040
The accuracy of the Stukas' attacks was devastating and demoralizing,

94
00:09:26,041 --> 00:09:31,640
but a simple makeshift device further heightened Vestuka's psychological effect on the enemy.

95
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:39,640
They had pipes actually, simple pipes from cardboard paper at the side of the bombs

96
00:09:39,641 --> 00:09:44,080
and these pipes made a terrible noise like a siren you know

97
00:09:44,081 --> 00:09:47,620
and that was in the beginning in the Polish war particularly

98
00:09:47,621 --> 00:09:51,640
and the troops heard those sirens they put the noise down in the dirt.

99
00:09:56,040 --> 00:10:02,380
Medium bombers like the Heinkel He-111 bombed the airfields of the obsolete Polish Air Force.

100
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:07,140
What aircraft did make it into combat were no match for the guns of the Luftwaffe.

101
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:11,960
In two days, Goring's Air Force had won complete supremacy of the sky.

102
00:10:16,340 --> 00:10:21,540
The destruction of road and rail networks stopped Polish reinforcements from reaching the front.

103
00:10:21,541 --> 00:10:31,600
a success and rapidity of the advance staggered even the germans in eight days the panzer divisions

104
00:10:31,601 --> 00:10:37,700
had reached the outskirts of warsaw a few days later the last polish counter-offensive had been

105
00:10:37,701 --> 00:10:45,660
halted and destroyed on september 27th the polish capital surrendered the first lightning campaign

106
00:10:45,661 --> 00:10:46,374
was over.

107
00:10:46,375 --> 00:10:52,560
The German armed forces learned wonderful lessons in the execution of combined

108
00:10:52,561 --> 00:10:55,580
ground air tactics in the assault on Poland.

109
00:10:55,581 --> 00:10:59,080
The aircraft that they had were perfect for the job

110
00:10:59,081 --> 00:11:01,481
and they were opposed by a brave enemy.

111
00:11:01,482 --> 00:11:04,560
The Polish troops were brave, but totally unsuited

112
00:11:04,561 --> 00:11:05,760
for modern warfare.

113
00:11:05,761 --> 00:11:10,120
In the first two days of fighting on the eastern front, the Luftwaffe

114
00:11:10,121 --> 00:11:12,480
or destroyed 2,000 Russian aircraft.

115
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:15,880
The Luftwaffe will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

116
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:23,980
On Germany's western border, the Maginot Line stood as France's only defense against the threat of invasion.

117
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:27,200
A maze of bunkers and heavy gun emplacements,

118
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:32,400
the line was built in a time when armies moved into combat by foot and on horseback.

119
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:38,640
Nothing, the French believed, could pass in front of the guns of the Maginot Line and survive.

120
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:45,880
But Hitler had no intention of passing within the range of French guns.

121
00:11:45,881 --> 00:11:52,000
His strategy, Plan Yellow, would exploit the mobility of his army to outflank the fixed

122
00:11:52,001 --> 00:11:56,900
defenses and attack the Maginot Line from the rear.

123
00:11:56,901 --> 00:12:03,120
At first light on May 10, 1940, 74 divisions of Hitler's army thrust through Belgium and

124
00:12:03,121 --> 00:12:05,320
Holland.

125
00:12:05,321 --> 00:12:09,500
The main thrust of the attack against Holland came from the Luftwaffe.

126
00:12:09,501 --> 00:12:15,400
bombers, including the Junkers' Ju-88, began with the now familiar tactic of destroying

127
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:16,660
enemy airfields.

128
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:25,560
The Ju-88 could carry over three tons of high-explosive bombs distributed between its internal bomb

129
00:12:25,561 --> 00:12:27,260
bay and wing pylons.

130
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:33,920
Its excellent speed, great maneuverability, and immense structural strength made the Ju-88

131
00:12:33,921 --> 00:12:36,320
a rugged and accurate dive bomber.

132
00:12:36,321 --> 00:12:42,860
But light defensive armament made this twin-engined bomber vulnerable to enemy fighter attack.

133
00:12:46,620 --> 00:12:55,840
As bombers pounded the Dutch countryside, a fleet of 475 three-engine Junkers Ju-52 troop transports swept into the country.

134
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:06,040
Before the war, the Ju-52 was used as a civilian airliner.

135
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:13,500
The Junkers transport planes brought to the battlefield a new combat force, paratroopers.

136
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:22,040
The aircraft flew in a tight formation of three aircraft at 150 miles per hour, 400 feet above the ground.

137
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:26,940
Speed was reduced to about 100 miles per hour for the men to jump.

138
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:31,720
After they were out, containers full of arms and ammunition were also dropped.

139
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:37,920
German paratroops landed on air bases, supply areas, and key bridges throughout the small country.

140
00:13:38,720 --> 00:13:44,120
By early afternoon, 1,200 airborne soldiers had won control of the major airfields.

141
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:51,200
By May 13th, only the city of Rotterdam remained in Dutch control.

142
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:56,500
Göring ordered the bombing of Rotterdam as a show of force to hasten the surrender of the Dutch.

143
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:00,000
The heart of the city was completely destroyed.

144
00:14:00,001 --> 00:14:07,000
Armored columns drove through Belgium almost unopposed and in two days Hitler's panzers reached the French border.

145
00:14:07,001 --> 00:14:18,000
The Germans crossed the Meuse River. Blitzkrieg poured into France.

146
00:14:18,001 --> 00:14:24,000
The German shock troops faced a disorganized enemy.

147
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:31,000
The German shock troops faced a disorganized enemy.

148
00:14:31,001 --> 00:14:34,000
The Luftwaffe quickly gained air superiority.

149
00:14:34,001 --> 00:14:42,000
As they had done in the battle for Poland, Ju-87 Stuka dive bombers hunted and attacked.

150
00:14:42,001 --> 00:14:53,000
When they encountered heavy resistance, German ground forces called on the Stukas to bomb targets with the accuracy of flying artillery.

151
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,720
Luftwaffe radio operators provided the valuable link.

152
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:05,100
We created the connection between the army and the flying units.

153
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:10,740
There was a time when the close communication between ground troops and the air force was developed.

154
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:17,700
And unfortunately, when we came to Dunkirk, where all this was supposed to take place,

155
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:19,780
we couldn't put it to work because of the weather.

156
00:15:20,540 --> 00:15:26,640
The advancing Germans pushed the retreating French soldiers and British forces to the port town of Dunkirk.

157
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:32,740
With victory only moments away, Hitler ordered his panzers to halt on the outskirts of the city.

158
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:43,720
The army was held back in front of Dunkirk because Goering had promised that the Luftwaffe would take care of the whole affair of Dunkirk.

159
00:15:43,721 --> 00:15:54,140
But then bad weather came up and the Luftwaffe was not able to bomb as much as would have been necessary to keep the British troops in Dunkirk.

160
00:15:54,680 --> 00:15:58,160
To save their army, the British launched Operation Dynamo.

161
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:06,100
Starting on May 26, anything that could float sailed from England across the channel to France and plucked the beleaguered army from the beaches.

162
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:13,340
For 10 days, the Luftwaffe made a determined effort to bomb the stranded troops and sink ships sailing across the channel.

163
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:55,727
The Royal Navy rescued over 238,000 British soldiers and 100,000 French troops, escaping what Winston Churchill feared would be the greatest military disaster in

164
00:16:55,728 --> 00:16:56,920
British history.

165
00:16:56,921 --> 00:17:01,160
They left everything there at the beach.

166
00:17:01,460 --> 00:17:08,540
Their cars, one car next to the other, almost on top of each other, and a tremendous amount of material.

167
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:16,780
I still remember going there to the beach and finding everything from toothbrushes to sheet music,

168
00:17:17,120 --> 00:17:20,360
and they just took their bare life and got out of there.

169
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:26,280
The Luftwaffe had every reason to be extraordinarily pleased with itself.

170
00:17:26,281 --> 00:17:28,960
It had simply overwhelmed all of its opponents.

171
00:17:29,740 --> 00:17:35,780
In every instance, its airplanes had been superior to the enemies, its pilots had had better training, its tactics were more effective.

172
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:42,100
It was at Dunkirk, however, that the Luftwaffe really came up for the first time against the Royal Air Force flying the Spitfires.

173
00:17:49,900 --> 00:17:55,620
For the first time, the Germans had an inkling that now this is going to be an opponent of different quality.

174
00:17:55,621 --> 00:18:01,240
We're not going to have the easy walkway that we had in all the previous combinations.

175
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:05,780
In the bright sunshine of June 21, 1940,

176
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:10,720
Hitler and Goering met to accept the surrender of the French in the forest of Compiègne.

177
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:18,000
The old railway car was the same car in which the French had accepted the German surrender to end World War I.

178
00:18:19,140 --> 00:18:23,260
Hitler and his blitzkrieg had carried the sword of vengeance into France.

179
00:18:23,261 --> 00:18:28,060
Variations of the Messerschmitt BF-109 design

180
00:18:28,061 --> 00:18:30,840
were produced by the Avia Company in Czechoslovakia

181
00:18:30,841 --> 00:18:33,980
and the Hispano Company of Spain until 1957

182
00:18:33,981 --> 00:18:36,520
Modern models will return in a moment

183
00:18:36,521 --> 00:18:41,120
When France was defeated, finally

184
00:18:41,121 --> 00:18:43,660
Hitler and Gehring thought England would surrender

185
00:18:43,661 --> 00:18:46,540
So Hitler actually went off on a month's vacation

186
00:18:46,541 --> 00:18:49,100
He toured the battlefields, Gehring began looting

187
00:18:49,101 --> 00:18:51,800
and the Bluff Waffe began refitting an equipment

188
00:18:51,801 --> 00:19:00,620
It was like a gift of life to the Royal Air Force because it had suffered heavy losses in France, and now it had a chance to rebuild.

189
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:07,120
British industry quickly raised production of their front-line fighters to defend against a possible German invasion.

190
00:19:07,860 --> 00:19:16,100
But before Hitler could land troops on English beaches, Goring and his Luftwaffe would have to destroy the Royal Air Force and win air superiority.

191
00:19:16,101 --> 00:19:20,880
Goering gathered all his forces and it was a formidable force.

192
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:23,840
It had been perfect for Poland, been perfect for France.

193
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:25,960
It was not the right force for the Battle of Britain.

194
00:19:26,660 --> 00:19:32,840
Goering counted on his best fighter, the Messerschmitt BF-109, to blast the Spitfires out of the sky.

195
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,920
The Messerschmitt had fought with the Luftwaffe since the first blitzkrieg against Poland.

196
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:41,980
The fast BF-109 made mincemeat of its opposition.

197
00:19:41,981 --> 00:19:46,980
But taking the fight to England presented an unforeseen problem.

198
00:19:46,981 --> 00:19:52,980
They had to take off from bases in France, fly across the channel, and when they got into battle,

199
00:19:52,981 --> 00:19:56,980
they perhaps had 10 or 15 minutes of combat time before they had to leave.

200
00:19:56,981 --> 00:20:00,980
And then they had to fly back across the channel with their emergency fuel light blinking red.

201
00:20:00,981 --> 00:20:05,980
The Luftwaffe's main assault against the RAF began on August 13th.

202
00:20:05,981 --> 00:20:06,681
13th.

203
00:20:06,682 --> 00:20:14,020
Codenamed Adler-Tag, Eagle Day, the operation called for the bombing of RAF airfields

204
00:20:14,021 --> 00:20:16,220
and radar stations along the English coast.

205
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:23,391
The Spitfire and the Messerschmitt were very, very comparable.

206
00:20:23,392 --> 00:20:24,960
The Spitfire was slightly

207
00:20:24,961 --> 00:20:27,479
faster and slightly more maneuverable.

208
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:30,660
The Messerschmitt had a better rate of climb and

209
00:20:30,661 --> 00:20:32,324
a much better diving capability.

210
00:20:32,325 --> 00:20:35,340
And it usually amounted to which pilot was the best pilot.

211
00:20:52,957 --> 00:20:57,380
With the ground radars giving them the course of the incoming bombers,

212
00:20:57,381 --> 00:21:03,640
the Spitfires and Hawker Hurricanes wasted neither time nor fuel hunting the skies for the formations.

213
00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:12,980
Pilot's bill decided the result of individual dogfights.

214
00:21:12,981 --> 00:21:16,980
The British had the advantage of fighting over home territory.

215
00:21:16,981 --> 00:21:19,980
Damaged aircraft were never far from a friendly base.

216
00:21:19,981 --> 00:21:24,980
The shot down pilot was often back in action within hours.

217
00:21:35,980 --> 00:21:49,760
By the end of August, disappointed with mounting losses,

218
00:21:49,940 --> 00:21:55,460
Goring ordered his fighters and bombers to concentrate their attacks against RAF airfields.

219
00:21:56,220 --> 00:21:59,960
The Luftwaffe had not succeeded in blinding British Fighter Command.

220
00:21:59,961 --> 00:22:03,760
The radar network was restored and remained intact.

221
00:22:05,980 --> 00:22:12,740
The bomber and fighter attacks against the airfields cost the RAF their greatest casualties.

222
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:19,540
In two weeks, the RAF had lost 295 fighters destroyed at 171 damaged.

223
00:22:20,180 --> 00:22:24,920
But far more serious was the loss of 103 pilots killed or missing.

224
00:22:24,921 --> 00:22:38,921
The escorting BF-109s fought savagely to protect the slow, ungainly Stukas and twin-engine bombers.

225
00:22:41,220 --> 00:22:44,240
The legend of the Stuka perished over England.

226
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:50,920
Despite losing 378 aircraft, the Luftwaffe continued attacks against the airfields.

227
00:22:50,921 --> 00:22:59,500
In retaliation for a small British bombing raid on Berlin, Hitler, in a fury, ordered

228
00:22:59,501 --> 00:23:08,080
the Luftwaffe against London.

229
00:23:08,081 --> 00:23:13,400
On September 7th, the bombers turned away from the RAF airfields and attacked the civilian

230
00:23:13,401 --> 00:23:15,940
population of East London.

231
00:23:15,941 --> 00:23:20,100
This marked the turning point of the whole battle.

232
00:23:20,101 --> 00:23:24,340
fighter pilots no longer had to fight over their airfields and could concentrate on the

233
00:23:24,341 --> 00:23:27,180
great armadas of bombers making their way to London.

234
00:23:37,260 --> 00:23:43,040
Without a long-range fighter escort, the Messerschmitts had to operate at the limit of their range

235
00:23:43,041 --> 00:23:46,860
and could defend the bombers for only a few minutes over London.

236
00:23:46,861 --> 00:23:52,060
On September 15th, vast formations of bombers were attacked.

237
00:23:52,720 --> 00:24:00,120
The loss of 60 German aircraft proved to Goring that the goal of air superiority was as far away as ever.

238
00:24:02,580 --> 00:24:05,400
The Germans knew that they had failed on their task.

239
00:24:05,800 --> 00:24:08,580
They knew that they were not going to gain air superiority.

240
00:24:08,581 --> 00:24:17,879
But even after the Battle of Britain, even after they had suffered their losses, German leadership did not see its way clear to expanding production or to changing the

241
00:24:17,880 --> 00:24:22,390
types. They persevered in the same weaponry and in the same levels of production.

242
00:24:22,391 --> 00:24:26,900
It's an absolutely unanswerable question why the right conclusions weren't drawn.

243
00:24:26,901 --> 00:24:36,960
As we could see it, the propaganda made it as non-exist or I would say, well it was not so decisive.

244
00:24:37,900 --> 00:24:41,820
We still were in the feeling that we still had the upper hand in it.

245
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:44,980
Of course, we had no clue.

246
00:24:47,340 --> 00:24:51,340
But losing the Battle of Britain was by no means the end of the assault.

247
00:24:51,960 --> 00:24:55,600
The bombing of England continued, under cover of darkness.

248
00:24:55,601 --> 00:25:02,100
Over London, when I flew, that was unbelievable how it was going on there as far as flak and balloons.

249
00:25:02,940 --> 00:25:04,920
And you saw it started burning.

250
00:25:05,160 --> 00:25:14,540
It was just like rolling out a carpet the way those little bombs would develop and would start fires below.

251
00:25:15,540 --> 00:25:20,040
But even the Blitz of London did not destroy the British will to resist.

252
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:26,040
Hitler now counted on his submarine force to sink freighters and starve the British into submission.

253
00:25:28,940 --> 00:25:36,120
Göring surrendered to Admiral Karl Donitz, chief of the German Navy, the control of a wing of reconnaissance aircraft.

254
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:42,180
With these four-engine Focke-Wulf 200s, Donitz could expand his search for enemy shipping.

255
00:25:42,181 --> 00:25:50,500
The Condor, originally designed as a 26-seat passenger airliner, had a range of over 2,200 miles.

256
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:57,480
In 1938, the Japanese asked that one be built for their navy as a maritime reconnaissance aircraft.

257
00:25:58,120 --> 00:26:04,520
That plane proved so successful for the Japanese that the Luftwaffe adapted the aircraft for the same mission.

258
00:26:05,340 --> 00:26:10,300
They carried a bomb and a variety of machine guns, even while on reconnaissance missions.

259
00:26:11,180 --> 00:26:16,800
These long-range aircraft could not only sink stragglers, but vector available U-boats to the convoys.

260
00:26:35,046 --> 00:26:41,680
initially their navigation reports could be off by as much as 80 miles but by keeping the condor

261
00:26:41,681 --> 00:26:46,780
on station, orbiting over the target, the U-boats could find the slow-moving freighters.

262
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:55,100
By the winter of 1940, the Condors had sunk over 90,000 tons of Allied shipping.

263
00:26:56,020 --> 00:27:01,800
For the next three years, the Condors were, in Churchill's words, the scourge of the Atlantic.

264
00:27:03,940 --> 00:27:09,420
The fundamental failing of the Condor was structural weakness and catastrophic wing

265
00:27:09,421 --> 00:27:14,380
failures not only while in flight but even on the ground.

266
00:27:14,381 --> 00:27:19,380
That and the limited payload capability of the Fokker Wolf 200 prevented it from becoming

267
00:27:19,381 --> 00:27:26,380
the aircraft that the Luftwaffe desperately required, a long-range heavy bomber.

268
00:27:26,381 --> 00:27:33,060
Picasso's masterpiece, Guernica, was painted in protest to the Luftwaffe bombing of the

269
00:27:33,061 --> 00:27:36,020
vast village during the Spanish Civil War.

270
00:27:36,021 --> 00:27:38,640
The Luftwaffe will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

271
00:27:40,140 --> 00:27:48,420
Although weakened by the losses of the Battle of Britain, the Luftwaffe was being committed to one full-scale offensive after another.

272
00:27:49,420 --> 00:27:55,800
By late spring of 1941, German plans called for the invasion of the Mediterranean island of Crete.

273
00:27:57,060 --> 00:28:03,120
British and New Zealander forces, after surrendering Greece to the German invaders, had fled south to the island.

274
00:28:03,121 --> 00:28:14,114
The Luftwaffe gambled that by using all available paratroopers together, they could strike the island in force, overwhelm the 30,000 defenders and capture the three

275
00:28:14,115 --> 00:28:15,120
main airfields.

276
00:28:15,121 --> 00:28:24,120
Once the paratroopers had taken the airfields, the Junkers 52 transport aircraft would return with infantry and artillery support.

277
00:28:24,121 --> 00:28:26,027
artillery support.

278
00:28:26,028 --> 00:28:34,080
Operation Mercure, Mercury, began on the morning of May 20th as the sky over

279
00:28:34,081 --> 00:28:38,746
Crete blossomed with a thousand parachutes.

280
00:28:38,747 --> 00:28:43,520
At first, the very nature of the attack, its

281
00:28:43,521 --> 00:28:47,716
terrifying unfamiliarity, had thrown the defenders off balance.

282
00:28:47,717 --> 00:28:49,780
But within minutes of the first

283
00:28:49,781 --> 00:28:53,637
landings paratroopers were met by a hail of small arms fire.

284
00:28:53,638 --> 00:28:55,180
Several of the parachute

285
00:28:55,181 --> 00:28:58,754
units were wiped out before they hit the ground.

286
00:28:58,755 --> 00:29:02,180
By late afternoon, it looked like the invading

287
00:29:02,380 --> 00:29:04,379
force would be annihilated.

288
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:08,820
The Luftwaffe dive bombed and strafed the anti-aircraft guns

289
00:29:08,821 --> 00:29:12,664
near the airfields and disrupted organized resistance.

290
00:29:12,665 --> 00:29:14,800
Transport returned with ground

291
00:29:14,801 --> 00:29:21,800
troops to reinforce the German positions.

292
00:29:22,300 --> 00:29:26,540
But when the British defenders regrouped, the Germans gained a precarious hold on

293
00:29:26,541 --> 00:29:32,540
one of the airfields.

294
00:29:32,541 --> 00:29:38,480
On May 26th, the British abandoned their positions and sailed off the island.

295
00:29:38,481 --> 00:29:40,740
The victory was costly.

296
00:29:40,741 --> 00:29:45,840
Of the 8,500 airborne soldiers, 3,700 had been killed on the first day.

297
00:29:46,640 --> 00:29:49,840
220 of the Junkers' transports were destroyed.

298
00:29:51,700 --> 00:29:58,200
The price of the invasion startled Hitler to such an extent that he would never again authorize such an operation.

299
00:29:59,240 --> 00:30:05,160
Crete was an Allied defeat, but it was also the grave of the Luftwaffe paratroops.

300
00:30:05,161 --> 00:30:15,780
barely a month later the Luftwaffe prepared more than half of its entire frontline force of 4300

301
00:30:15,781 --> 00:30:21,480
aircraft for the largest clash of arms in German history the invasion of Russia

302
00:30:21,481 --> 00:30:35,481
the attack on the soviet union had been authorized by hitler several months before

303
00:30:35,660 --> 00:30:41,160
and they disguised the movement of german troops and german airplanes to the eastern front quite

304
00:30:41,161 --> 00:30:48,320
well even though stalin had been advised by the british that the attack was coming and even though

305
00:30:48,321 --> 00:30:52,740
The Soviet commanders had been warned. Nothing was done to protect the airplanes.

306
00:30:53,480 --> 00:31:04,960
On Sunday morning, June 22, 1941, 117 German armored and infantry divisions rolled forward on a 1,200-mile battlefront into the Soviet Union.

307
00:31:07,720 --> 00:31:12,800
The Luftwaffe had destroyed 1,200 Russian planes by noon.

308
00:31:12,801 --> 00:31:18,300
In the next several days, the numbers destroyed went down, 800 to 600 to 300,

309
00:31:18,780 --> 00:31:21,840
not because the Russians were fighting any better,

310
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:25,300
but because there were simply less airplanes available to shoot up.

311
00:31:31,460 --> 00:31:36,860
By late November, Army and Air Forces had pushed within 19 miles of Moscow.

312
00:31:37,660 --> 00:31:41,200
But this was destined to be the limit of the German advance.

313
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:53,800
When the winter came, we had tremendous difficulties with ice and snow and no clothing, and it was a terrible experience.

314
00:31:54,480 --> 00:31:59,180
We lost all our equipment, we lost all our trucks, we were not prepared at all.

315
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:07,180
The invasion that Hitler boasted would be over in eight weeks was now bleeding his army of men and materiel.

316
00:32:08,180 --> 00:32:11,300
Ragged, freezing, and with their supply lines overextended,

317
00:32:11,960 --> 00:32:16,040
the Germans battled a seemingly endless stream of Russian infantry and armor.

318
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:25,260
Russian counteroffensives began to lever the Germans away from Moscow.

319
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:37,060
What the Germans were unaware of is that the Russians had a vast manufacturing base in the Urals.

320
00:32:37,180 --> 00:32:42,180
that they had transported the aircraft factories almost brick by brick on a train,

321
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:43,380
tunneling them across the country,

322
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:48,500
and they were able to begin re-erecting the factory just within days of their getting there.

323
00:32:51,640 --> 00:32:53,820
Within three months of the German invasion,

324
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:56,640
these factories were once again manufacturing airplanes.

325
00:32:57,280 --> 00:32:59,300
The Germans had bitten off more than they could chew.

326
00:32:59,301 --> 00:33:03,200
They had no idea of the extent and the strength of the Soviet Union.

327
00:33:03,201 --> 00:33:08,780
New, more capable Russian aircraft began to fly against the Luftwaffe.

328
00:33:09,520 --> 00:33:12,820
The German Air Force was beginning to lose air superiority.

329
00:33:14,100 --> 00:33:19,200
The Russians induced in great numbers the Ilyushin IL-2, the tank buster.

330
00:33:19,680 --> 00:33:22,400
And this was an airplane that did better than the Stuka.

331
00:33:22,740 --> 00:33:25,560
It was virtually invulnerable. It was truly a flying tank.

332
00:33:26,240 --> 00:33:31,100
The Ilyushin Stormovik ground attack aircraft tore into the German armor.

333
00:33:31,880 --> 00:33:38,520
The Stormovics attacked with rockets and heavy guns and made life on Earth hell for the infantry.

334
00:33:39,320 --> 00:33:40,649
And they built them in great numbers.

335
00:33:40,650 --> 00:33:42,660
They built something like 36,000 of them during the war.

336
00:33:43,100 --> 00:33:45,880
There were only about 8,000 Stukas built during the war.

337
00:33:46,440 --> 00:33:55,400
For the first time, it became apparent that the production of Luftwaffe aircraft was inadequate to sustain a long period of heavy air operations.

338
00:33:55,401 --> 00:34:02,400
Support of Field Marshal Erwin Rummel's Africa Corps spread even thinner the Luftwaffe's meager resources.

339
00:34:02,401 --> 00:34:12,049
The Desert Foxes' fight to regain territory lost to the British pulled away aircraft and pilots that were desperately needed on the frozen battlefields in the Soviet

340
00:34:12,050 --> 00:34:12,750
Union.

341
00:34:12,751 --> 00:34:20,400
The Luftwaffe became a fire brigade constantly on emergency call to salvage hard-pressed army units.

342
00:34:20,401 --> 00:34:28,094
Sometimes they would have to land in no man's land and offload the supplies and then the Germans who were besieged would have to fight their way out to get the

343
00:34:28,095 --> 00:34:29,400
supplies from the Russians.

344
00:34:29,401 --> 00:34:38,400
To move army troops and their supplies quickly along the thin front line, the Luftwaffe employed a most unusual aircraft.

345
00:34:38,401 --> 00:34:47,400
The Messerschmitt 323 could lift more than 22 tons of cargo, enough to handle all but the heaviest of German armor.

346
00:34:47,401 --> 00:34:48,101
armor.

347
00:34:48,102 --> 00:34:55,100
Two 7.9 millimeter machine guns in the nose and two mounted behind the wings provided

348
00:34:55,101 --> 00:35:09,101
protection for the four-man crew. This Leviathan first flew in 1940 as a glider.

349
00:35:11,803 --> 00:35:14,100
In a system

350
00:35:14,101 --> 00:35:19,020
known as the Troika Schlepp, three bombers pulling together would tow the plane into

351
00:35:19,021 --> 00:35:19,721
the air.

352
00:35:19,722 --> 00:35:25,780
This configuration proved impractical and the Messerschmitt's 323s were fitted with

353
00:35:25,781 --> 00:35:27,653
six radial engines.

354
00:35:27,654 --> 00:35:34,160
Its slow cruising speed of 140 miles an hour and immense size made

355
00:35:34,161 --> 00:35:37,280
it an easy target.

356
00:35:37,281 --> 00:35:49,180
On one disastrous mission flown without fighter escort, 14 of the 323s from a flight of 16 were shot down.

357
00:35:54,860 --> 00:36:02,340
By the winter of 1942, the German army concentrated its power on the destruction of the Red Army at Stalingrad.

358
00:36:02,341 --> 00:36:06,300
But their advances were turned back by the Soviet counterattacks.

359
00:36:07,060 --> 00:36:09,120
The Germans found themselves surrounded.

360
00:36:13,720 --> 00:36:18,620
General von Paulus surrendered his army on January 31st, 1943.

361
00:36:19,720 --> 00:36:24,660
It was the turning point of the war in the east and removed any hope of a German victory.

362
00:36:25,760 --> 00:36:31,100
The defeat at Stalingrad demonstrated that the Luftwaffe still adhered to the battle tactics of Blitzkrieg

363
00:36:31,101 --> 00:36:33,080
in close support of ground forces.

364
00:36:33,860 --> 00:36:37,560
These tactics, brilliantly successful in Poland and France,

365
00:36:38,100 --> 00:36:41,060
failed in the great battles of Operation Barbarossa.

366
00:36:42,860 --> 00:36:47,780
Where they were totally lacking was in the ability to reach beyond the front,

367
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:51,680
reach to the Russian manufacturing centers in the Urals and elsewhere,

368
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:52,780
and destroy them.

369
00:36:52,781 --> 00:36:55,440
They simply didn't have the airplanes to do it, nor did they have the will to do it.

370
00:36:55,560 --> 00:36:56,660
They ignored it.

371
00:36:56,661 --> 00:37:06,820
The Luftwaffe lost 488 transport planes and 1,000 crew members in a vain attempt to supply the encircled German army at Stalingrad.

372
00:37:07,440 --> 00:37:08,860
Modern Marvel to return in the moment.

373
00:37:18,260 --> 00:37:23,760
The morning of December 7, 1941, sealed the fate of the Luftwaffe.

374
00:37:24,740 --> 00:37:33,480
Honoring a pact signed with the Japanese, Hitler and Goring found their Third Reich at war with the most powerful industrial nation in the world.

375
00:37:33,700 --> 00:37:40,980
I remember one day when my father said, America ended the war and we should quit right here and then.

376
00:37:41,360 --> 00:37:46,560
This is the end for us because we cannot match their material superiority.

377
00:37:46,561 --> 00:37:56,260
When it became apparent that the Allies were securing both a quantitative and a qualitative advantage, the Germans then turned back to their jet proposals.

378
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:02,100
We heard some strange noise overhead.

379
00:38:02,500 --> 00:38:09,100
If we did see anything, it was just a glimpse of some look like an aircraft, but it didn't sound like an aircraft.

380
00:38:09,101 --> 00:38:13,540
So we asked the flight instructors, what in the hell is this up there?

381
00:38:13,541 --> 00:38:22,801
It was top secret and we were told not to ask questions, not to shoot at those, since they showed us the shadow models, you know.

382
00:38:22,802 --> 00:38:28,400
If you see something like that flying, don't shoot at it. It's ours, you know.

383
00:38:28,401 --> 00:38:33,540
Armed with four 30-millimeter cannon and underwing rockets,

384
00:38:33,820 --> 00:38:37,580
the Messerschmitt Me 262 proved a formidable aircraft.

385
00:38:38,200 --> 00:38:41,520
It could not be caught by any Allied fighter in a straight chase

386
00:38:41,521 --> 00:38:44,600
and could decimate bomber formations with its heavy cannons.

387
00:38:46,200 --> 00:38:51,740
Now, the Messerschmitt 262 was designed as a fighter aircraft.

388
00:38:51,741 --> 00:39:00,100
However, by order of Hitler himself, we are to fly the 262 as a bomber.

389
00:39:00,500 --> 00:39:06,560
Nobody had thought that anyone would want to use this aircraft as a bomber.

390
00:39:07,640 --> 00:39:14,600
His concept was that if he had perhaps a couple of hundred ME-262 bombers on D-Day,

391
00:39:15,020 --> 00:39:17,200
that they could have disrupted the landings on the beaches.

392
00:39:17,201 --> 00:39:22,380
And that's not a bad thought, but it was a misapplication of the airplane.

393
00:39:22,540 --> 00:39:24,480
It didn't have a bomb site. It wasn't very accurate.

394
00:39:24,620 --> 00:39:30,740
And it could have been very effective if they'd been manufactured in quantities and used against the bomber formations.

395
00:39:30,960 --> 00:39:37,540
Although the Germans built almost 1,300 of the aircraft, only 300 of them ever got into operational situations.

396
00:39:37,720 --> 00:39:39,040
Most of them destroyed on the ground.

397
00:39:39,041 --> 00:39:40,717
Throughout 1943, U.S.

398
00:39:40,718 --> 00:39:52,300
bomber forces, flying from their bases in England, developed their daylight offensive with pinpoint attacks against German war-making industries.

399
00:39:53,900 --> 00:39:57,220
The Luftwaffe turned its full attention to the defense of the Reich.

400
00:39:57,221 --> 00:40:09,420
The task at hand was to do whatever we can to stop the incoming aircraft from reaching their targets.

401
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:12,740
The odds were overwhelming against us.

402
00:40:12,741 --> 00:40:21,060
In my group, 27 aircraft would take off and we would get where we could see them.

403
00:40:21,061 --> 00:40:31,940
we then knew that we were outnumbered maybe 10 to 1 maybe 20 to 1 or even more we had orders not

404
00:40:31,941 --> 00:40:41,860
to ever engage accompanying p-51s we were to use our time in the air and our ammunition strictly

405
00:40:41,861 --> 00:40:44,320
to get down bombers.

406
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:48,340
The only way you can really count on being successful

407
00:40:48,341 --> 00:40:54,580
is if you go to the point where your gun sight is black,

408
00:40:55,220 --> 00:41:00,180
filled with the enemy aircraft, and then open fire.

409
00:41:06,460 --> 00:41:10,280
Desperate young men with only a few hours in the cockpit of a fighter plane

410
00:41:10,281 --> 00:41:12,740
were sent into the cauldron boiling over Germany.

411
00:41:14,160 --> 00:41:17,160
I was just a little past my 16th birthday.

412
00:41:17,820 --> 00:41:23,320
The pressures and the stresses were very, very great.

413
00:41:23,321 --> 00:41:31,360
I recall being almost at the point of shaking from fear

414
00:41:31,361 --> 00:41:37,320
as we were approaching the enemy bomber groups that were coming in.

415
00:41:37,321 --> 00:41:49,560
It was then no longer a matter of being a person out of flesh and blood with feelings and all that.

416
00:41:50,020 --> 00:41:53,440
It was just like I turned into a machine.

417
00:41:54,580 --> 00:41:59,820
It was always 100 or 1,000 aircraft coming in over Germany.

418
00:41:59,821 --> 00:42:09,060
and here we were, 12, 20, maybe sometimes a few more, trying to do something to stop them.

419
00:42:11,800 --> 00:42:19,040
By 1944 and early 1945, it was quite evident that it was hopeless.

420
00:42:29,820 --> 00:42:32,640
One thousand years of unreal human behavior.

421
00:42:33,240 --> 00:42:37,880
Hitler, Osama, Hussein, ten madmen who terrorized the world.

422
00:42:38,360 --> 00:42:40,720
Attila, Ivan, Caligula.

423
00:42:41,460 --> 00:42:42,980
One week of sheer madness.

424
00:42:43,580 --> 00:42:45,520
They too are barbarians.

425
00:42:45,521 --> 00:42:48,480
All this week as only the History Channel can bring you.

